    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7699769237,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-07ef65257636",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": -1234.2186718748558,
      "y": 2431.625,
      "strokeColor": "#0F172A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 646.316650390625,
      "height": 585.0,
      "seed": 367016,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Apache Iceberg\nHow does Iceberg work, and how do you use it? Start from Snowflake. One\nproduct there owns three jobs: it stores the bytes, it tracks the table metadata,\nand it runs the compute. Iceberg breaks those three jobs apart and standardizes\nthe middle one. Your rows sit in ordinary Parquet files in object storage that you\nown. S3 is the common choice, but GCS, ADLS, HDFS, and MinIO work the\nsame way; Iceberg is not S3-native and uses no proprietary storage. Next to\nthose data files, Iceberg keeps a small set of metadata files. They record which\nParquet files form the table right now, the schema, the partition rules, and per-file\nstatistics such as row counts and column minimum and maximum values. That\nmetadata is the table format. Iceberg is a written specification, so it runs no\nqueries and stores no bytes itself.\n\nTwo external parts complete the picture. A catalog holds one pointer per table:\nthe location of the table's current metadata file. Each commit writes new files,\nthen swaps that pointer in one atomic step. This swap is what makes a write\nACID, and it is what gives you snapshots and time travel. Common catalogs are\nthe Iceberg REST catalog, AWS Glue, Apache Polaris, Project Nessie, Unity\nCatalog, and Snowflake Open Catalog. An engine is the compute. It asks the\ncatalog for the pointer, reads the metadata, skips the files that cannot match the\nquery, and runs the SQL. Spark, Trino, Flink, DuckDB, BigQuery, Athena, and\nSnowflake can all act as the engine. The payoff is that several engines share one\ncopy of the data, with no exports and no copies. Your work stays SQL and\nmodeling, so a dbt model can materialize as an Iceberg table. You keep the\nSnowflake behavior you expect, such as atomic commits, time travel, and\nschema evolution, on files you own and any engine can read.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-07ef65257636",
      "originalText": "Apache Iceberg\nHow does Iceberg work, and how do you use it? Start from Snowflake. One product there owns three jobs: it stores the bytes, it tracks the table metadata, and it runs the compute. Iceberg breaks those three jobs apart and standardizes the middle one. Your rows sit in ordinary Parquet files in object storage that you own. S3 is the common choice, but GCS, ADLS, HDFS, and MinIO work the same way; Iceberg is not S3-native and uses no proprietary storage. Next to those data files, Iceberg keeps a small set of metadata files. They record which Parquet files form the table right now, the schema, the partition rules, and per-file statistics such as row counts and column minimum and maximum values. That metadata is the table format. Iceberg is a written specification, so it runs no queries and stores no bytes itself.\n\nTwo external parts complete the picture. A catalog holds one pointer per table: the location of the table's current metadata file. Each commit writes new files, then swaps that pointer in one atomic step. This swap is what makes a write ACID, and it is what gives you snapshots and time travel. Common catalogs are the Iceberg REST catalog, AWS Glue, Apache Polaris, Project Nessie, Unity Catalog, and Snowflake Open Catalog. An engine is the compute. It asks the catalog for the pointer, reads the metadata, skips the files that cannot match the query, and runs the SQL. Spark, Trino, Flink, DuckDB, BigQuery, Athena, and Snowflake can all act as the engine. The payoff is that several engines share one copy of the data, with no exports and no copies. Your work stays SQL and modeling, so a dbt model can materialize as an Iceberg table. You keep the Snowflake behavior you expect, such as atomic commits, time travel, and schema evolution, on files you own and any engine can read.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "apache-iceberg"
      }
    },
