        "node_id": "metadata-not-in-catalog"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7126380939,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-6ec2c47324d8",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1900.0,
      "y": 5873.75,
      "strokeColor": "#92400E",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 315.0,
      "seed": 110084,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Metadata Is Not in the Catalog\nThe metadata file, the manifest list, and the\nmanifests are real files in your object\nstorage, beside the data files. The catalog\nstores only the path of the current metadata\nfile. This split exists because only the\npointer needs atomic replacement; everything\nelse is immutable, so engines read it straight\nfrom storage in parallel. Two cases blur the\nline. A catalog can return the metadata\ncontents to a client, because it read the file\nfor you, and it may cache them; the file stays\nthe source of truth. A managed service can\nalso hide the files from view, but they still\nexist in your storage. If you come from a\nwarehouse, expect the word to mislead you:\nthere a catalog is a rich metadata service,\nhere it is a pointer holder.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-6ec2c47324d8",
      "originalText": "Metadata Is Not in the Catalog\nThe metadata file, the manifest list, and the manifests are real files in your object storage, beside the data files. The catalog stores only the path of the current metadata file. This split exists because only the pointer needs atomic replacement; everything else is immutable, so engines read it straight from storage in parallel. Two cases blur the line. A catalog can return the metadata contents to a client, because it read the file for you, and it may cache them; the file stays the source of truth. A managed service can also hide the files from view, but they still exist in your storage. If you come from a warehouse, expect the word to mislead you: there a catalog is a rich metadata service, here it is a pointer holder.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "metadata-not-in-catalog"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
